  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nCurrently, clouds and fog cleared across the region by mid/late \nmorning and temperatures across the forecast area have rebounded \nnicely. Afternoon highs for the area will reach into the lower 60s \nfor central Kansas with middle to upper 50s expected for southeast \nKansas where clouds hung on a little longer. A weak mid/upper level \ntrough extends a cross the Plains and is gradually shifting to the \neast. A faint surface trough will move across the state later this \nevening and into the overnight hours turning light winds out of the \nnorth by morning. Mostly clear skies and light winds overnight \nthrough early morning will allow for notable radiational cooling \nleading to patchy fog across south-central and southeastern Kansas. \nAs the coolest part of the airmass shifts off to the east, only \nsoutheast Kansas will dip cool enough to have patchy frost concerns \nfor Sunday morning.\n\nFor Sunday into the middle of next week, broad ridging will build in \nover the western and central portions of the country. Southerly to \nsoutheasterly flow will support a gradual warmup across the area. \nHighs each afternoon through Wednesday will top out in the upper 60s \nand lower 70s with overnight lows generally falling into the 40s \nareawide. Mostly clear conditions are expected, however overnight \nradiational cooling may continue to lead to patchy fog across\nsouthern portions of the state each morning. \n\nBy mid week, a deep trough will begin to dig into the Northeast US \nwith the forecast area remaining on the western periphery of the \ntrough. The better dynamics and moisture look to remain just to our \neast at this time, limiting our precip chances with this feature. A \ncold front will push across the eastern half of the country bringing \nmore seasonable temperatures to the Central Plains. Temperatures for \nthe end of the forecast period will be slightly cooler with highs \ntopping out in the upper 50s and lower 60s and potentially dipping \ninto the 30s once again.",
